# CMake stays in sync by globbing, not by hand

`CMakeLists.txt` collects sources, headers and shaders with
`file(GLOB_RECURSE ... CONFIGURE_DEPENDS)` under `DXI/`. A new `.cpp`, `.h` or `.hlsl`
anywhere in that tree is picked up automatically. **Do not hand-list files.**

## After adding, renaming or deleting any file

Always build, and expect the first build to fail:

- `CONFIGURE_DEPENDS` re-globs at build time, *after* MSBuild has already loaded the
  project, so the new file is missing from the current build. A new `.cpp` fails at link
  with unresolved externals, a deleted one fails with `C1083: Cannot open source file`.
- **Build a second time.** It passes. This lag is inherent to globbing with the Visual
  Studio generator, it is not a mistake to chase.
- If a second build still fails, reconfigure:
  `cmake --build <build-dir> --target DXIApp` after `cmake <source-dir>`.

## When CMakeLists *does* need editing

Globbing only covers what the patterns already match. Edit `CMakeLists.txt` when:

- a file uses an extension not in the patterns (sources: `.cpp .c .cc .cxx`,
  headers: `.h .hpp`, shaders under `DXI/shaders`: `.glsl .hlsl .comp .hip .cl`)
- shaders are added outside `DXI/shaders`, which is globbed separately from sources
- a third party dependency is introduced, which needs `FetchContent` plus either
  `target_sources` or `target_link_libraries`
- a new header folder must be on the include path — normally automatic, since include
  directories are derived from `DXI_HEADERS`, but `DXI/libs` is deliberately excluded

## Verify before reporting work complete

A change is not finished until `cmake --build` succeeds. State in the summary that the
build passed, and if a new file was added, that it passed on the rebuild.
